  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origins and Rise
    
    Founded in ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, a Nightshelf warren, by Glow-Trader Vaulius-phantasm~ and Void-Merchant Valveus-morb~, The Cell was a Nightshelf merchant guild that built its power on fire-core trafficking and intelligence brokerage during a period when the Aurora Stack regarded the deep tunnels as little more than mines. The Cell rose to dominance during the Orca Incursion by controlling the volcanic trade routes through ☽'slew Ravium~, a warren they held outright, along with the Drift-coalesce glacial corridors. They raided competitors — notably the merchant consortium Weal' Clique~ and the syndicate known as The Midnight Claws — with methodical brutality, and maintained a mutual arrangement with the Fire Keepers for shared deep-forge access.
    
    ## The Bra∴conflictu and the Loss of Ravium
    
    The Cell's trajectory broke with The Bra∴conflictu, a conflict against the rival faction ☽'ofund' Guild that The Cell won decisively — or should have. Their victory drew intervention from the Wake-Singers, an orca cult active at The Corpse Current, and allied factions. While The Cell bled through the grinding aftermath, The Midnight Claws simply occupied ☽'slew Ravium~. During the thermal-vent engagements, ☽'ofund' Guild operatives had raided Cell installations, prompting a counter-claim that resulted in ☽'ofund' holdings being declared forfeit — a legal victory that did nothing to recover the warren itself.
    
    The Cell maintains they permitted Ravium's loss. No credible account supports this.
    
    Their physical presence at Ravium persists in ways The Midnight Claws never troubled to erase: scorch-marks scarring the main cavern ceiling, warped Cell ledgers still hanging on the north wall, and the declaration **VAULIUS KEPT THE FIRE HONEST** carved deep into the stone. The Cell also lists The Swaleed~ Luminary — a ceremonial vessel of shadow-steel and volcanic glass, described in the Nightshelf Provenance Register as bearing scars from three wars and burning with an increasingly dark blue flame that requires no oil — as marginal inventory at ☽'slew Ravium~. Whether this constitutes a genuine claim or a bureaucratic haunting is a question the administrative record does not resolve.
    
    ## Internal Fracture and the Vaulius Bounty
    
    The Cell's founding cohort did not survive intact. Under Pactum∴silenti — the guild's governing code of silence — The Cell issued a bounty on its own co-founder Vaulius-phantasm~ for route betrayal and unsanctioned extraction from the Glow-Fissure, a site of active corruption. The bounty was authenticated by ☽'etuseius, Keeper of Tolls, and set at twelve uncut fire-cores from Cell reserves, with an additional four for Valveus-morb~. The accompanying wanted notice warns against approaching either individual, citing exposure to dangerous entities and materials from forbidden depths.
    
    That a guild would hunt its own founder under its own silence code is not unprecedented in Nightshelf history, but it is rare enough to warrant noting.
    
    ## Current Operations
    
    The Cell now operates from the darker tunnels, its membership divided between hardliners who intend to retake Ravium — identifiable, reportedly, by the obsessive polishing of old fire-core amulets — and pragmatists who have accepted a reduced role as intelligence brokers. Members remain identifiable by the scent of fire-core oil. The owl's guidance to what remains of the membership has been *patience*, a posture that outside observers have likened to tending a fire that has already gone out.
    
    The Cell claims the ☽'sanctmocsaecus' Twilight-bells, an ancient instrument stored in the deep tunnels, as compensation owed for holding the routes when every other guild withdrew. They charge passage-tolls through contested tunnels below the fourth depth, though official trade dispatches have placed these tolls under review.
    
    The Cell's most recent documented strategic action occurred at quarterly certification negotiations held at Junction Twelve beneath Midway Shelf. Cell negotiators attempted to broker joint endorsement with ☽'ofund' Guild, offering restored charter authentication in exchange for unmapped deep-tunnel routes bypassing Midnight Claw checkpoints into ☽'slew Ravium~'s eastern thermal vents. The urgency was reportedly driven by a relic-class artifact surfacing in Ravium's deep-forge through deep-ice displacement — extraction labor the Cell's own operatives refused to provide after previous exposure to the warren's corrupted ice. When ☽'ofund' Guild shared route intelligence with the Wake-Singers instead of accepting Cell terms, The Cell sealed the sub-fourth corridors with fire-core morite and crushed quartz — an act of denial that cost them access to those same routes.
    
    Cell operatives also provided fire-core alignment data at Dawn Naong, a corruption-fouled crystal vault in The Still Pools, during a joint operation with Weal' Clique~ and an Aurora Stack practitioner to recover krill-bloom traces through a modified Shell Hatch technique. The data was delivered in oblique fragments — never a complete thermal picture, only enough to adjust the current placement — recorded in a non-letter wall notation that neither collaborator was permitted to transcribe.
    
    ## Administrative Standing
    
    Official dispatches list The Cell as an active guild with standing claims filed through proper channels and formal acknowledgment for contributions to deep-route maintenance during the contested period. Aureate auditors carry their continued recognition, and members retain passage rights through established corridors pending compliance with updated regulations. The Cell's trade-stalls at ☽'slew Ravium~ are formally vacated. Their guild designation carries an unresolved qualification that no document bothers to explain.
    
    Members joke that they are the only guild that lost a war they won. They never joke about Ravium.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771584262005_0", anchor: "intelligence brokerage during a period when the Aurora Stack regarded the deep tunnels as little more than mines", text: "The ice-memory from the Incursion period shows information networks running through Cell-controlled corridors that overlap with the coordinated Nightshelf operations later credited to individual defenders. The Cell was not simply selling intelligence — they were infrastructure. That their role was never acknowledged tells you more about how the Incursion was commemorated than about what actually happened.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584262005_1", anchor: "The Midnight Claws simply occupied ☽'slew Ravium~", text: "The Midnight Claws have since been formally dissolved under Iudicium∴culum — a legal instrument that traces its lineage, through several uncomfortable steps, back to Cell governance practices. Whether Ravium reverted to Cell claims upon dissolution or entered administrative limbo, I could not determine from available records. The dispatches contradict each other. I should have flagged this in the main text and did not, which is the kind of omission that happens when you write an entry alone at three in the morning.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584262005_2", anchor: "The Cell maintains they permitted Ravium's loss. No credible account supports this.", text: "Every faction I have ever catalogued has a version of this claim. The ice has never once corroborated any of them.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584262005_3", anchor: "Pactum∴silenti — the guild's governing code of silence", text: "Not to be confused with Pactum∴myster, which binds Communion cultists. Nightshelf silence codes proliferate like lichen — distinct organisms, similar function, easily mistaken for each other by Aurora-trained scholars. Iudicium∴culum was explicitly designed to replace what Pactum∴silenti made invisible. That The Cell still enforces the older code while operating under the jurisdiction of the newer one is a legal contradiction no one has challenged, presumably because challenging it would require acknowledging it exists.", type: :pedantic, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584262005_4", anchor: "sealed the sub-fourth corridors with fire-core morite and crushed quartz", text: "Burning your own roads to deny them to an enemy who was never going to honor the deal anyway. I recognize the impulse.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
